  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use a multi-resolution strategy that combines smooth constrained optimization, gradient-based learning from good parent solutions, and sudden jumps to explore different functional shapes. This approach directly addresses the challenge of optimizing a non-convex function with box and integral constraints. The key innovation is the use of a smooth approximation function that replaces the non-differentiable max operation in the objective, enabling efficient gradient-based optimization. By optimizing at several resolutions and transferring structures across scales, we balance exploration and exploitation while ensuring feasibility.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe optimize the continuous step function over [0,2] with:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n- Objective: min max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) (1 - h(x+k)) dx\n\nDiscretized:\n- n_point samples: x_i \u2208 [0,2]\n- dx = 2/n_points\n- Objective: min C5 = max\u2096 \u2211 h_i \u00b7 (1 - h_{i+k}) \u00b7 dx\n\nRepresentation:\n`h_values` is a real-valued function vector of length n_points in [0,1] (not necessarily binary). The provided `initial_h_values` (n=500) is used with manifold projection for transfer.\n\nOptimization formulation:\nWe formulate as constrained minimization using the smooth valley construction:\nsmooth_f(h, n, \u03b1) = (m + (1/\u03b1) \u00b7 log(\u2211 exp(\u03b1\u00b7(c - m)))) \u00d7 dx\nwhere c is the raw autocorrelation, m = max(c), and \u03b1 is a smoothing parameter (initially 1000).\n\nWe require:\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n- \u2211 h_i = n/2\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Use provided `initial_h_values` (or fallback to `binarize` if unavailable)\n   - Project to feasible space via `project_simplex` (clipping + iterative adjustment)\n   - For new resolution, transfer solution via 3rd-order interpolation\n\n2. Optimization per resolution:\n   use_low = {\"maxiter\": 500}\n   use_high = {\"maxiter\": 1000}\n   sol = minimize(\n      smooth_obj,         # Smoothed objective function\n      h0,                 # Initial solution\n      bounds=[(0,1)]*n, \n      constraints=eq_sum,\n      method='SLSQP',\n      options={\"ftol\": 1e-12, **use_low}\n   )\n   # If failed, try progression of options\n\n3. Objective evaluation:\n   score = compute_c5(sol.x, n_points) * dx\n   fd_check = abs(sol.x.max() - 1) + abs(sol.x.min()) > 1e-5\n   if (feasible and improved_score):\n      store_candidate(n_points, sol.x, score)\n\n4. Validation:\n   Feasibility check: all(h <= 1) and abs(sum(h)*dx - 0.5) < 1e-5\n   Use multiple dx values (sample multiple lags) for more robust score\n\n5. Resolution hopping:\n   Sequence = [500, 600, 800, 1000]\n   For each n in next_resolution:\n     transfer_solution = interplace_h(best_solution_from_500, n)\n     run_time = estimate(n) (O(n log n) per iteration)\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- alpha (smoothing parameter): starts at 1000, decreases by 10% per iteration if plateau detected (fall below)\n- Dynamic maxiter: 500 for n=500, 1000 for n>500\n- Dimensionality: Must adjust bounds and initial samples based on dimension\n- Temperature schedule: Cooling rate based on progress in score\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Time estimate: O(n log n) per optimization iteration, with ~200-300 iterations\n- Starting at n=500: ~1 dimension might require ~0.5 seconds/iteration\n- Higher n needs more iterations proportionally to function evaluations\n- Deadline monitoring: Use nested timer checks at start of each optimization\n- Restart strategy: If plateau > 0.3*maxiter with minimal score improvement, adjust alpha by 2x random away from current\n- Fallback: Project best found solution via simplex projection if loss of feasibility\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Feasibility: 0<=h<=1 + sum(h)\u2248 n/2 (within 1e-5)\n- Score: Compute_c5 with multi-sample check (2nd and last lags)\n- Format: (h_values, final_score, n_points)\n- Check object identity: Ensure h is not clipped/quantized during storage",
